Year = {2026},
Volume = {16},
Number = {3},
Month = {JUL},
Abstract = {Given the increasing awareness regarding sustainable environmental
   development in Saudi Arabia and considering children's invaluable role
   in fostering a sustainable future. This study explored preschool
   children's environment-related perceptions and practices, particularly
   concerning consumption patterns, recycling, and reuse. The study was
   guided by constructivism, which emphasizes children's active role in
   building knowledge through interactions with their environment, and
   Bronfenbrenner's ecological systems theory, which highlights the
   multiple contextual influences shaping children's development. The
   sample comprised 17 boys and girls aged 4 to 6 children and 16 of their
   mothers. Employing a qualitative study design, semi-structured
   interviews were conducted with the children and open-ended
   questionnaires were administered to their mothers. Results revealed that
   the children were more conscious of some consumption behaviors than
   others and generally had a high level of understanding of recycling and
   reuse techniques, attributable to the influence of their parents and the
   home environment. This study provides valuable insights into children's
   knowledge and comprehension of important sustainability concerns for
   teachers, supervisors, and curriculum developers seeking to enhance
   children's understanding of environmental issues and conservation
   methods during their formative years.},
